		<main class="container">
			<div class="docs-layout">
				<aside class="sidebar">
					<h3>Getting Started</h3>
					<ul>
						<li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
						<li><a href="#installation">Installation</a></li>
						<li><a href="#quick-start">Quick Start</a></li>
					</ul>

					<h3>Cloud Relay</h3>
					<ul>
						<li><a href="#expose">Expose Command</a></li>
						<li><a href="#self-hosting">Self-Hosting Relay</a></li>
					</ul>

					<h3>Local Tunneling</h3>
					<ul>
						<li><a href="#start">Local Tunnels</a></li>
						<li><a href="#daemon">Daemon Mode</a></li>
						<li><a href="#helper">Privileged Helper</a></li>
					</ul>

					<h3>Reference</h3>
					<ul>
						<li><a href="#cli">CLI Reference</a></li>
						<li><a href="#env-vars">Environment Variables</a></li>
					</ul>

					<h3>Troubleshooting</h3>
					<ul>
						<li><a href="#issues">Common Issues</a></li>
					</ul>
				</aside>

				<div class="content">
					<h1 id="overview">gotunnel Documentation</h1>

					<p>gotunnel is an open-source, self-hostable tunneling tool. Expose local dev servers to the internet via a relay server, or create local HTTPS domains — all from one CLI.</p>

					<div class="alert alert-info">
						<strong>Two modes:</strong> Use <code>gotunnel expose</code> for public URLs (like ngrok, self-hosted). Use <code>gotunnel start</code> for local .local domains (HTTPS on your machine).
					</div>

					<h2 id="installation">Installation</h2>

					<p><strong>Homebrew (macOS/Linux):</strong></p>
					<pre><code>brew tap v1truv1us/gotunnel
brew install gotunnel</code></pre>

					<p><strong>Go Install:</strong></p>
					<pre><code>go install github.com/v1truv1us/gotunnel/cmd/gotunnel@latest</code></pre>

					<p><strong>Install Script:</strong></p>
					<pre><code>curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/v1truv1us/gotunnel/main/scripts/install.sh | bash</code></pre>

					<h2 id="quick-start">Quick Start</h2>

					<h3>Option A: Cloud Relay (public URL)</h3>
					<p>Requires a relay server (see <a href="#self-hosting">Self-Hosting</a> below). Works from any device.</p>
					<pre><code>gotunnel expose -p 3000 -d myapp --relay tunnel.yourdomain.com

# Output:
#   Local:   http://localhost:3000
#   Public:  https://myapp.tunnel.yourdomain.com</code></pre>

					<h3>Option B: Local Tunnel (HTTPS on your machine)</h3>
					<p>No server needed. One-time setup for port-free URLs:</p>
					<pre><code># One-time: install privileged helper (port 80/443 forwarding + DNS)
sudo gotunnel install-helper

# Start a tunnel
gotunnel start -p 3000 -d myapp

# Output:
#   Access:  https://myapp.local</code></pre>

					<h2 id="expose">Expose Command (Cloud Relay)</h2>

					<p>Connects to a relay server via WebSocket. The relay forwards public traffic to your local machine.</p>
					<pre><code>gotunnel expose -p 3000 -d myapp --relay tunnel.yourdomain.com</code></pre>

					<p><strong>Flags:</strong></p>
					<ul>
						<li><code>-p, --port</code> — local port to expose (default: 8080)</li>
						<li><code>-d, --domain</code> — subdomain for the public URL</li>
						<li><code>-r, --relay</code> — relay server URL</li>
					</ul>

					<p>Auto-reconnects on disconnect. Press Ctrl+C to stop.</p>

					<h2 id="self-hosting">Self-Hosting the Relay</h2>

					<p>Deploy the relay server on your own infrastructure. Works with Docker, Coolify, or any VPS.</p>

					<h3>Docker</h3>
					<pre><code>docker build -f Dockerfile.relay -t gotunnel-relay .
docker run -p 8080:8080 -e RELAY_DOMAIN=tunnel.example.com gotunnel-relay</code></pre>

					<h3>Coolify</h3>
					<ol>
						<li>Create project → Dockerfile → path: <code>/Dockerfile.relay</code></li>
						<li>Set domain: <code>tunnel.yourdomain.com</code></li>
						<li>Enable wildcard domain: <code>*.{`{domain}`}</code></li>
						<li>Set env var: <code>RELAY_DOMAIN=tunnel.yourdomain.com</code></li>
						<li>Deploy</li>
					</ol>

					<h3>DNS Setup</h3>
					<pre><code>A    tunnel.yourdomain.com     → server IP
A    *.tunnel.yourdomain.com  → server IP</code></pre>

					<h3>Relay API</h3>
					<ul>
						<li><code>GET /health</code> — health check</li>
						<li><code>GET /api/tunnels</code> — list connected tunnels</li>
						<li><code>WS /ws?domain=...</code> — WebSocket tunnel endpoint</li>
					</ul>

					<h2 id="start">Local Tunnels</h2>

					<p>Create local HTTPS domains for development. Uses <code>.local</code> with SNI-based TLS proxy and mkcert certificates.</p>
					<pre><code># Start daemon (auto-starts on first tunnel)
gotunnel daemon

# Create tunnels
gotunnel start -p 3000 -d frontend    # → https://frontend.local
gotunnel start -p 8080 -d api         # → https://api.local

# Manage
gotunnel list                         # Show active tunnels
gotunnel stop frontend                # Stop specific tunnel
gotunnel stop-all                     # Stop everything</code></pre>

					<h2 id="daemon">Daemon Mode</h2>

					<p>The daemon runs persistently with a macOS menu bar icon. Tunnels survive terminal closure.</p>
					<pre><code># Start daemon manually (or it auto-starts on first 'gotunnel start')
gotunnel daemon

# Menu bar shows:
#   🚇 Active tunnels (2)
#   ● https://frontend.local     [click to copy]
#   ● https://api.local          [click to copy]
#   ⏹ Stop frontend
#   ⏹ Stop api
#   ⏹ Stop All
#   Quit gotunnel</code></pre>

					<p>The daemon auto-starts when you run <code>gotunnel start</code> if it's not already running.</p>

					<h2 id="helper">Privileged Helper</h2>

					<p>One-time setup for port-free URLs (80→8080, 443→8443) and automatic <code>/etc/hosts</code> management.</p>
					<pre><code># Install (one-time, macOS/Linux)
sudo gotunnel install-helper

# Uninstall
sudo gotunnel uninstall-helper</code></pre>

					<p>The helper runs as a system service (launchd on macOS, systemd on Linux) and:
					<ul>
						<li>Forwards port 80→8080 and 443→8443</li>
						<li>Manages /etc/hosts entries for .local domains</li>
						<li>Resolves *.local DNS queries for network devices</li>
					</ul></p>

					<h2 id="cli">CLI Reference</h2>

					<pre><code>COMMANDS:
   expose    Expose a local port via relay server
   start     Start a local HTTPS tunnel
   stop      Stop a specific tunnel
   list      List active tunnels
   stop-all  Stop all tunnels
   daemon    Start daemon with menu bar icon
   install-helper    Install privileged port forwarder (sudo)
   uninstall-helper  Remove privileged port forwarder (sudo)

GLOBAL OPTIONS:
   --proxy value        Proxy mode: builtin, auto, none [$GOTUNNEL_PROXY]
   --proxy-http-port    HTTP port (default: 8080)
   --proxy-https-port   HTTPS port (default: 8443)
   --debug              Enable debug logging [$DEBUG]
   --sentry-dsn         Sentry DSN (opt-in) [$GOTUNNEL_SENTRY_DSN]</code></pre>

					<h2 id="env-vars">Environment Variables</h2>

					<table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1rem 0;">
						<thead>
							<tr style="background: var(--bg-light);">
								<th style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border); text-align: left;">Variable</th>
								<th style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border); text-align: left;">Description</th>
								<th style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border); text-align: left;">Default</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);"><code>RELAY_DOMAIN</code></td>
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Base domain for relay server</td>
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);">—</td>
							</tr>
							<tr style="background: var(--bg-light);">
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);"><code>RELAY_ADDR</code></td>
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Relay listen address</td>
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);"><code>:8080</code></td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);"><code>GOTUNNEL_PROXY</code></td>
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Local proxy mode</td>
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);"><code>auto</code></td>
							</tr>
							<tr style="background: var(--bg-light);">
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);"><code>GOTUNNEL_SENTRY_DSN</code></td>
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Sentry error tracking (opt-in)</td>
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);">disabled</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);"><code>DEBUG</code></td>
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Enable debug logging</td>
								<td style="padding: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--border);"><code>false</code></td>
							</tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>

					<h2 id="issues">Common Issues</h2>

					<h3>Mobile device can't resolve .local domain</h3>
					<p>iOS browsers don't resolve <code>.local</code> via mDNS. Use the relay server (<code>gotunnel expose</code>) for mobile testing, or access via the Mac's IP address.</p>

					<h3>Certificate warning in browser</h3>
					<p>gotunnel uses self-signed certificates via mkcert. To trust them locally:</p>
					<pre><code>mkcert -install</code></pre>
					<p>For mobile devices, accept the certificate warning or use the relay server (which uses your server's TLS).</p>

					<h3>Port 8080 already in use</h3>
					<p>Another process is using the proxy port. Use <code>--proxy-http-port 9080</code> to change it, or <code>--proxy=none</code> to skip the proxy.</p>

					<h3>Relay connection refused</h3>
					<p>Check that the relay server is running and the domain is correct. The client auto-reconnects every 3 seconds.</p>

					<div class="alert alert-info">
						<strong>Need help?</strong> Open an issue on <a href="https://github.com/v1truv1us/gotunnel/issues">GitHub</a>.
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</main>
	</body>
</html>